77dc36a88eed2a"}, {"id": "377b5d", "text": "Resonance in ac circuits", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed2b"}, {"id": "717085", "text": "Mutual induction in ac circuits", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed2c"}, {"id": "94547f", "text": "interference of electromagnetic waves", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed2d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed2e", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfae7"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b88"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95ca"], "question": {"text": "A circuit element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math> when connected to an AC&nbsp;supply of peak voltage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> gives a peak current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> which is in phase with the voltage. A second element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math> when connected to the same AC&nbsp;supply also gives the same value of peak current which lags behind the voltage by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math> are connected in series to the same supply, what will be the rms value of the current in ampere?"}, "options": [{"id": "e6caca", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>10</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed2f"}, {"id": "4f2a92", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed30"}, {"id": "c4c2a0", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed31"}, {"id": "a89168", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed32"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed33", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfae7"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475c"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4712"], "question": {"text": "A coil of negligible resistance is connected in series with $90 \\Omega$ resistor across $120 \\mathrm{~V}, 60 \\mathrm{~Hz}$ supply. A voltmeter reads $36 \\mathrm{~V}$ across resistance. Inductance of the coil is :"}, "options": [{"id": "3ddac7", "text": "$0.286 \\mathrm{H}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed34"}, {"id": "f3f869", "text": "$0.76 \\mathrm{H}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed35"}, {"id": "4eb7f0", "text": "$2.86 \\mathrm{H}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed36"}, {"id": "cd4f98", "text": "$0.91 \\mathrm{H}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed37"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed38", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfae7"], "topics": ["60e5673b499d1e49588f8b88"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95ca"], "question": {"text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> voltage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#969;</mi><mi>t</mi></math>&nbsp;of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi></math> is applied to a parallel plate capacitor. The separation between the plates is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> and the area is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. The amplitude of the oscillating displacement current for the applied <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math> voltage is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mtext>Take&#160;</mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "0e4053", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>21</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>&#956;A</mtext></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed39"}, {"id": "35a22b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>83</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>&#956;A</mtext></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed3a"}, {"id": "a7588e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>27</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>79</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>&#956;A</mtext></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed3b"}, {"id": "145b30", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>55</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>&#956;A</mtext></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed3c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4ef1c677dc36a88eed3d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60ced3330e34b73944ddfae7"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b475c"], "concepts": ["60e40fd4f1ef260f84ed1e46"], "question": {"text": "<p>In the given circuit, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></math>&nbsp;value of current <math xmlns